A sim is a perfect sandbox: free resets, clean reward, near-zero cost per try. Shi first ran autoresearch one weekend on a pushT sim, with code as the policy and no neural network. The real world has none of that, so he showed two ways to close the loop, plus a new exam.

1️⃣ ENPIRE runs autoresearch directly on real robots. From 50 demos and an empty codebase, the agent builds a fixed 
http://
env.py (reset, reward, observation, step) from human feedback, then hillclimbs on its own: 8 robots, 8 RTX 5090s, 8 Codex orchestrators, ~20 minutes per idea.

2️⃣ Result: 99% pass@8 on PushT, pin insertion, GPU insertion and two ziptie tasks.

3️⃣ SimEX simulates first and adapts in the real world. With zero demos, a coding agent builds a reusable toolbox in a roughly correct simulator, then a few real trials correct the sim and the toolbox together. About 10 minutes of real interaction was enough for towel folding, barcode scanning and plate manipulation.

4️⃣ The Embodied First Exam (EFE): 1,156 tasks, 2,288 tests, 35 suites, 47 robots. The agent may drive the robot, write control code or train a policy, and an external verifier grades it. On 456 core tasks, the strongest agent (GPT-6 Astra in Codex) reaches ~34% in the Standard setting.

5️⃣ Model-based vs. model-free dissolves: system ID, real2sim, VLAs, RL and control theory all become tools an agent can call. His last slide: "Goosebumps: a Paradigm Shift is Occurring in Robotics."

My read:
• pass@8 means up to 8 memory-conditioned retries per subtask in one rollout. It measures recovery, not first-try success, and no pass@1 or cycle time is reported.
• ENPIRE's labeled ideas add up to only 17.2 pp, while a "Re-evaluate" step is worth +12.5 pp. Evaluation noise is as large as most algorithmic gains, so a hillclimber can select noise.
• 8× the robots bought ~2.25× wall-clock: about 28% parallel efficiency.
• EFE's core set was filtered with GPT-6 Luna, tilting it against GPT models, so Astra's ~34% is likely conservative. SimEX still needs per-task numbers against a no-sim control.

The hard part of physical autoresearch is reset and verification, not the model. Congrats to my co-organizers 
@_wenlixiao
 and 
@letian_fu
, who are on the ENPIRE author list.

